{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.68,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U2"],
      "pins": ["U2.VI", "U2.VO"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "VCC"],
      "observed": "On the regulator sheet, U2 (LM1117-5.0) has its input pin VI tied to the +5V net (the same power flag that feeds C3 and the MCU VCC pins) and its output pin VO also tied to +5V. The raw input rail VCC (from J3 'PWR' pin 2, which is the only source of VCC) is not connected to the regulator at all; the regulator's IN and OUT pins are shorted together on +5V.",
      "expected": "A 5 V linear regulator's input (VI) must be connected to the unregulated supply (VCC, the higher-voltage rail that also powers the fan through Q2/JP2), with only VO on +5V. As drawn, +5V has no source: the regulator cannot derive the logic rail from VCC.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U2 LM1117-5.0 with '+5V' flag on the VI side and '+5V' flag on the VO side (right tile, row A)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "J3 PWR pin 2 -> VCC (the only VCC source); U3/U4 pin 8 -> +5V (MCU supply on +5V)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Atmel/ ... 'an ATtiny-driven fan controller'"}
      ],
      "impact": "The +5V logic rail is never generated, so the ATtiny and all 5V logic (pot, I2C header, reset) are unpowered; only the fan's VCC power path is energized. The controller cannot boot or regulate the fan.",
      "confidence": 0.68
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The LM1117-5.0 regulator U2 is mis-wired: both its input (VI) and output (VO) sit on the +5V net and the raw VCC input from J3 is not connected to it, so the 5V logic rail has no source and the MCU never powers up."
}

